          "title": "You'll want to know: dēserō, dēserere",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dēserō, dēserere — \"to abandon, desert\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Amīcus vērus amīcum in perīculō nōn dēserit. A true friend does not abandon his friend in danger."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dēserō, dēserere, dēseruī, dēsertum: dē- 'away' + serō 'to join' — to break a tie. English desert."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dēserō is built on dē- \"away\" and serō \"to join, link\" (Lewis & Short): to break off a tie. English desert comes from its participle dēsertus."
            }
          ]
        },
